WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:00.860
What is an FDE?

2
00:00:01.513 --> 00:00:04.013
Forward Deployed Engineer.

3
00:00:04.013 --> 00:00:06.093
It's the hottest
job title in tech

4
00:00:06.093 --> 00:00:08.573
right now. And it
sounds way more

5
00:00:08.573 --> 00:00:11.133
mysterious than
it actually is.

6
00:00:11.702 --> 00:00:14.722
Picture an engineer
fired down a pneumatic

7
00:00:14.722 --> 00:00:17.182
tube, laptop
under one arm,

8
00:00:17.182 --> 00:00:19.122
straight into a
client's office.

9
00:00:19.618 --> 00:00:21.298
That's the job,
more or less.

10
00:00:21.756 --> 00:00:24.636
The company doesn't
just sell you software.

11
00:00:24.636 --> 00:00:27.216
It sends someone in
until the software

12
00:00:27.216 --> 00:00:28.316
actually works.

13
00:00:28.941 --> 00:00:31.721
Say an aircraft maker
buys your platform.

14
00:00:31.721 --> 00:00:33.661
Parts data in one system.

15
00:00:33.661 --> 00:00:35.501
Maintenance logs
in three more.

16
00:00:35.983 --> 00:00:40.323
An ERP from 2009
nobody dares touch.

17
00:00:40.323 --> 00:00:43.643
And a spreadsheet called
FINAL_v7_REAL that quietly

18
00:00:43.643 --> 00:00:45.003
runs the whole company.

19
00:00:45.515 --> 00:00:47.115
You can't hand
them a login and

20
00:00:47.115 --> 00:00:50.295
leave. Someone has to
untangle all of it.

21
00:00:50.815 --> 00:00:52.295
Enter the FDE.

22
00:00:52.295 --> 00:00:53.355
They move in for weeks,

23
00:00:53.355 --> 00:00:54.295
sometimes months.

24
00:00:55.241 --> 00:00:58.301
One Palantir engineer
spent a year at

25
00:00:58.301 --> 00:01:01.621
Airbus in Toulouse,
on the factory floor

26
00:01:01.621 --> 00:01:03.061
four days a week.

27
00:01:03.734 --> 00:01:06.354
They learn how the
place really runs,

28
00:01:06.354 --> 00:01:08.774
which is never what
the manual says.

29
00:01:09.351 --> 00:01:11.331
Then they connect
the systems,

30
00:01:11.331 --> 00:01:14.011
write the code and
ship the dashboards.

31
00:01:14.011 --> 00:01:15.791
Sometimes a whole feature,

32
00:01:15.791 --> 00:01:16.851
for one customer.

33
00:01:17.502 --> 00:01:18.962
So it's part engineer,

34
00:01:18.962 --> 00:01:21.602
part architect, part
product manager,

35
00:01:21.602 --> 00:01:25.142
part consultant. Palantir
has its own names

36
00:01:25.142 --> 00:01:25.502
for this.

37
00:01:26.093 --> 00:01:28.273
A Dev is one capability,

38
00:01:28.273 --> 00:01:32.253
many customers. A
Delta is one customer,

39
00:01:32.253 --> 00:01:34.173
many capabilities.

40
00:01:34.679 --> 00:01:36.639
And a Delta isn't
a consultant.

41
00:01:36.639 --> 00:01:39.599
A Delta builds, and
leaves working code,

42
00:01:39.599 --> 00:01:40.799
not a slide deck.

43
00:01:41.367 --> 00:01:42.907
Why work like this?

44
00:01:42.907 --> 00:01:44.867
Because working in
the demo and working

45
00:01:44.867 --> 00:01:47.767
at the customer are
different planets.

46
00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:50.540
Every company
has its own data,

47
00:01:50.540 --> 00:01:54.320
permissions, security
rules and strange rituals.

48
00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:56.720
The FDE closes that gap.

49
00:01:57.657 --> 00:01:59.077
And the bonus:

50
00:01:59.077 --> 00:02:01.197
when the same
fix turns up at

51
00:02:01.197 --> 00:02:04.197
five customers, it
becomes part of the

52
00:02:04.197 --> 00:02:06.737
product. That's how
a lot of Palantir's

53
00:02:06.737 --> 00:02:07.897
Foundry took shape.

54
00:02:08.482 --> 00:02:11.142
So why is it
exploding now? AI.

55
00:02:11.739 --> 00:02:13.679
Nobody wants
another chatbot.

56
00:02:13.679 --> 00:02:15.638
They want an agent
wired into their

57
00:02:15.638 --> 00:02:19.699
customer records, database,
documents and billing,

58
00:02:19.699 --> 00:02:22.639
safely. Someone
has to go in and

59
00:02:22.639 --> 00:02:23.359
wire it.

60
00:02:23.908 --> 00:02:25.168
And it's a lot.

61
00:02:25.168 --> 00:02:26.788
A Python bug
in the morning,

62
00:02:26.788 --> 00:02:28.348
the ops team after lunch,

63
00:02:28.348 --> 00:02:30.668
the product
redesigned by dinner.

64
00:02:31.304 --> 00:02:32.864
Easiest way to
remember it:

65
00:02:32.864 --> 00:02:34.464
an engineer builds it,

66
00:02:34.464 --> 00:02:36.084
a consultant explains it,

67
00:02:36.084 --> 00:02:38.564
an FDE moves
in and makes it

68
00:02:38.564 --> 00:02:40.584
work for your
exact problem.

69
00:02:41.054 --> 00:02:43.274
Comment DEPLOY and
I'll send you the

70
00:02:43.274 --> 00:02:44.614
one-page guide.

